#!/usr/bin/env etcs
#IMPORT ACE_ROOT/modules

# ETCS OVER A RING OF CYCLING DOTS -- the wait indicator.
#
#     run RenderProvider/scripts/throbber.etcs target=<some Drawable2D>
#
# and something to drive it, which is the frame edge that is almost certainly
# already running in any session that has a window:
#
#     detach RenderProvider/scripts/render_frames.etcs view=view
#
# The throbber claims Animated, so that one loop advances it along with every
# other animated leaf -- there is nothing per-throbber to start.
#
# AND ITS SPEED IS THAT LOOP'S SPEED. Advance() is per-visit for every animated
# leaf; this one simply does not spend the measured interval it is handed
# (RenderProvider/Throbber.h::AdvanceConcrete). RunFrames(16) is the default
# pace; RunFrames(4) spins it four times as fast along with everything else, and
# an edge that is struggling shows up as a spinner visibly slowing down. A
# throbber has no correct duration to keep, so there is nothing to be wrong --
# and "the session is labouring" is worth seeing on the one widget that is
# certainly on screen while you wait.
#
# ── what this script is for, and what it is not ──────────────────────────────
#
# ONE ENTITY DOES THE WHOLE JOB. spawn it, Create it at a size, and the label,
# the ring, the sweep and the colours are all inside (RenderProvider/Throbber.h
# on why the label is not a sibling this script places). So this file is a
# DEFAULT, not a construction: the two lines below are the whole of it, and a
# caller who wants a different size or rate is better off writing those two
# lines than passing this one a parameter.
#
# WHICH IT COULD NOT DO ANYWAY, and that is worth saying once here rather than
# being discovered: a run/detach binding is name=name only (core/Command.h
# parse_bindings), so there is no way to hand a script a NUMBER. Every widget
# script in this tree has literal numbers in it for that reason. The size here
# is 96 because that is the type's own default and the smallest size at which
# undithered discs still read as round.
#
# COORDINATES ARE THE TARGET'S, like every widget script here: SetPosition is in
# the pane's frame, so the subtree is relocatable because it was never absolute.
# Left at 0,0 -- a caller who wants it centred sizes a pane around it and puts
# the throbber in that, which is one number in one place instead of two that
# have to agree.

requires target [Drawable2D]

target.spawn(RenderProvider::Throbber throb)
throb.Create(96)

# Drawn over whatever the pane already holds. High rather than low: a wait
# indicator that something else covers is a wait indicator that is not there.
throb.SetOrder(90)

# Degrees of sweep per FRAME. 5.4 is the type's default and comes to 0.9
# revolutions a second at the frame edge's own default 16ms -- said here as the
# one line that shows the unit, since "per frame" is the whole of the timing
# choice above.
throb.SetStep(5.4)

# ── showing and hiding ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# SetHidden is the switch and there is no second one. Hidden, the throbber
# advances nothing (Throbber::AnimatingConcrete reads Hidden), so "not showing"
# and "not running" cannot disagree:
#
#     throb.SetHidden(1)      # gone, and costs one virtual call per frame
#     throb.SetHidden(0)      # back, sweeping from where it left off
#
# OR FOLLOW A FLAG. A type that is busy says so on itself -- a lowercase state
# tag, `busy` on PaintProvider's page store -- and the throbber can be bound to
# it, shown exactly while the tag is up, read once a frame on the edge:
#
#     throb.Watch(@pages, busy)
#     throb.Watch(@doc, syncing)   # a second watch: shown while EITHER is up
#     throb.Watch(@pages, )    # that one unbound; with none, SetHidden is the switch again
#
# While a watch is bound SetHidden is overruled a frame later, so a script that
# wants both binds the watch to the thing that knows.
#
# ── reaching a throbber somebody else already made ──────────────────────────
#
# A session that shows one at boot keeps it (PaintProvider's
# scripts/www/boot_paint_panels.etcs calls its `main_throbber`), and a subscript
# that wants a throbber should reach THAT one rather than adding a second to the
# tree. The verb for it is `ensure`, not `spawn`:
#
#     ensure RenderProvider::Throbber main_throbber
#     main_throbber.SetHidden(0)
#
# `spawn` always makes a new one and REFUSES a name that already resolves, so a
# subscript spelling it that way fails in exactly the session where the throbber
# was already there; `attach` takes the existing one but fails when there is
# none; `ensure` does the right thing either way (core/CommandExecutor.h,
# CmdAcquire). Which is why THIS script spawns -- it is the one that makes it --
# and a consumer ensures.
#
# Visible from here, because a throbber spawned hidden is the harder thing to
# debug: nothing appears and there is no way to tell whether the spawn failed or
# the hide worked. A caller that wants it hidden until something happens says so
# on the next line.
